  "answer_text": "How long sliced American cheese lasts after buying depends entirely on the **type** of American cheese you bought (individually wrapped singles vs. fresh deli slices) and **how it is stored**. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Individually Wrapped Processed Slices (e.g., Kraft Singles)\nBecause these slices are pasteurized, hermetically sealed, and contain preservatives, they have a remarkably long shelf life.\n\n* **Unopened Package:** Can last **1 to 2 months past the printed \"best-by\" or expiration date** in the refrigerator. \n* **Opened Package (Outer sleeve open, but slices remain in individual wrappers):** Will still last **1 to 2 months** in the refrigerator as long as the individual slice wrappers remain sealed. If you peel a slice out of its wrapper and leave it exposed, it will dry out and harden within a few days.\n* **In the Freezer:** You can freeze unopened packages for up to **3 months**. However, freezing is generally not recommended because it can make the cheese crumbly and hard to peel apart once thawed.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Sliced American Deli Cheese (Freshly sliced from the counter, e.g., Land O' Lakes)\nFreshly sliced deli American cheese is not individually wrapped and is typically packed in butcher paper or a simple zip-top bag. It has a much shorter shelf life because it is exposed to more air and moisture.\n\n* **In the Refrigerator:** It will last **2 to 3 weeks** after purchase if kept tightly sealed. However, for the absolute best quality and flavor, many deli brands recommend consuming it within **5 to 7 days** of purchase. \n* **In the Freezer:** Can be frozen for up to **8 months** if wrapped tightly in heavy-duty aluminum foil or plastic freezer wrap. (Like the singles, frozen deli cheese may become crumbly and is best used for melting or cooking once thawed).\n\n---\n\n### How to Tell If Sliced American Cheese Has Gone Bad\nAlways inspect your cheese before eating it. Discard it immediately if you notice:\n* **Mold:** Unlike hard block cheeses where you can cut mold off, you must **throw away** sliced cheese if you see any green, blue, or white mold, as mold spores easily travel through pre-sliced packages.\n* **Texture Changes:** Slices that are slimy, watery, or excessively dried out and cracked should be tossed.\n* **Bad Smell or Taste:** If it has a sour, yeasty, or off odor, it has spoiled and is no longer safe to eat. \n\n*Tip: To maximize shelf life, store your cheese in the middle or bottom shelves of the refrigerator\u2014not in the door, where temperature fluctuations from opening and closing can accelerate spoilage.*",
